\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated as per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), or trajectory-level patch scoring. Despite active development along each axis, no prior work compares these formulations under matched conditions. We present a systematic comparison across two model families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels, and multiple seeds, all at 4--8B LoRA fine-tuning scale.

At function level, all three formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy; Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) confirm per-test and trajectory approaches are statistically equivalent ($p{=}0.007$, $\pm$2\pp{} margin, 5 seeds). At repository level under the same training protocol, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with generation-based simulation falling below majority baseline. The pattern is consistent across model families but model-contingent in magnitude.

Two findings generalize. Per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity (which tests fail) at comparable accuracy, with configuration-dependent aggregation cost at repository level. Formulations exhibit complementary errors at function level (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%), motivating complexity-aware routing over uniform formulation selection.
\end{abstract}

