\section{Variant-Level Statistical Power}
\label{app:power}

Table~\ref{tab:variant_power} reports variant-level bootstrap confidence intervals and statistical power for the repo-level formulation comparisons ($n{=}48$ variants).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Variant-level bootstrap analysis ($n{=}48$). \cls{}--\cwm{} uses paired bootstrap (same variants, same seed); \cls{}--\traj{} uses unpaired bootstrap (per-variant \traj{} predictions not archived for pairing). Minimum detectable effect at $n{=}48$: 25.0\pp{}.}
\label{tab:variant_power}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Comparison} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (\pp{})} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} & \textbf{Power} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\emph{\cls{} vs.\ \cwm{} (paired)}} \\
& Qwen3-8B & +29.17 & [10.42, 47.92] & 0.003 & 0.784 \\
& DeepSeek-6.7B & +35.42 & [18.75, 50.05] & ${<}$0.001 & 0.960 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\emph{\cls{} vs.\ \traj{} (unpaired)}} \\
& Qwen3-4B & $-$4.17 & wide & 0.740 & 0.066 \\
& Qwen3-8B & +8.33 & wide & 0.385 & 0.163 \\
& DeepSeek-6.7B & 0.00 & wide & 1.000 & 0.025 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The \cls{}--\cwm{} gap is well-powered (mean power $= 0.87$) despite the small sample size ($n{=}48$), because the effect (29--35\pp{}) far exceeds the minimum detectable effect of 25.0\pp{}. The \cls{}--\traj{} comparisons are severely underpowered (mean power $= 0.08$): any true difference is too small to detect at this sample size, consistent with the function-level TOST equivalence (\S\ref{sec:equivalence}).

The \cls{}--\traj{} unpaired bootstrap produces wider confidence intervals than paired analysis would; \traj{} per-variant predictions were not archived in a format that permits pairing. The \cls{}--\cwm{} paired comparison uses \cls{} s123 vs.\ \cwm{} s42 (different seeds), but the CI lower bound of 10.42\pp{} far exceeds the seed noise observed in function-level experiments ($<$2\pp{}).

